#!/usr/bin/env python3
# ask-selftest — LAYER 0 of the Ask Bar: the thing that notices when a route dies.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, IN ONE SENTENCE: on 2026-09-06 three of five routes were dead for a whole
# day while the pill flashed green, because the only check anyone ran was "did ask print OK",
# and ask printed OK before it knew anything. A one-time check is what already failed here.
#
# 🔴 VERDICTS ARE PRINTED WORDS: PASS / FAIL / SKIP / ERROR. NEVER AN EXIT CODE.
#   This fleet has been bitten six times by exit-code-as-verdict, most recently
#   freeze_table.sh --check, whose codes are INVERTED (FROZEN=0, CLEAR=1). This script
#   always exits 0. The last line is always:  SELFTEST VERDICT: <WORD>
#   A scheduler that needs an exit status must derive it from that WORD, in exactly one
#   visible place -- see ask-selftest-cron, which is the only thing allowed to do that.
#
# 🔴 THE TWO FAILURE CLASSES IT COVERS, STATED SEPARATELY BECAUSE COLLAPSING THEM IS HOW
#    THE 2026-09-06 OUTAGE HID (CLAUDE.md Rule 8 clause 5):
#
#   CLASS A — "CAN THE COMMAND RUN?"  (this is F1, and it is what killed claude/codex/continue)
#       A1 shape       the row's own values are honourable: drop is HOST:/absolute, ask_cmd
#                      carries {q} and starts with an absolute path, vision is a real bool.
#       A2 drop-reach  the drop host answers, and the drop directory exists and is writable
#                      by the account `ask` copies as.
#       A3 cmd-resolve the ask_cmd executable is found on ask_host IN THE SAME NON-INTERACTIVE
#                      SHELL the dispatch uses. This is not a proxy for the failing lookup --
#                      it IS the failing lookup.
#       A5 drop-agentside  THE DROP HOST IS THE MACHINE THE AGENT READS ON, and that account
#                      can actually read the directory. A2 asks whether the file can LAND;
#                      this asks whether the reader is standing in the same place. They came
#                      apart on 2026-09-07: dreamzs dropped on Hub and read on GpuBox,
#                      every CLASS A check passed, and only an LLM call could see it. The
#                      registry now carries `agent_ssh` so this is decidable for free.
#       A4 cmd-health  the program actually STARTS and its credential is present. A3 alone is
#                      NOT this: `codex --version` exits 0 while `codex exec` dies on its own
#                      config, and `cn --version` exits 0 while cn is not logged in. Stage 1
#                      accepted `--version` as proof a route worked and Stage 2 refuted it.
#                      The probe comes from the ROW (`health:`), so a new agent brings its own.
#
#   CLASS B — "DID THE AGENT READ THE FILE?"  (this is F2/F3, and it is a DIFFERENT failure)
#       B1 round-trip  drop a file containing a token that exists NOWHERE ELSE plus two numbers
#                      to add, hand the agent only the PATH, and require both the token and the
#                      computed sum back. A polite answer, a bluff, or an agent that guessed a
#                      different path all fail this. Only --full runs it; it costs LLM calls.
#
#       B2 path-fidelity  DID IT READ *THE PATH WE NAMED*, or did it find the file some other
#                      way? The token alone cannot tell those apart -- dreamzs "worked" once by
#                      SEARCHING after the relative path it was handed did not resolve, and that
#                      recovery is what hid the bug. So two DECOY files, carrying different
#                      tokens and landing NEWER, are placed in the same drop directory and are
#                      never mentioned to the agent. An answer containing decoy content is proof
#                      the agent read a file it was never given the path to. Same LLM call as B1;
#                      the assertion is free.
#
#   🔴 A FOURTH VERDICT WORD: BLOCKED. A route waiting on a HUMAN is not a broken route, and
#   reporting both as FAIL is how a real breakage hides inside a known backlog -- three of five
#   routes are currently waiting on Chris. A probe declares `blocked_on:` WITH a `blocked_since:`
#   date; past its grace period (default 14 days) BLOCKED escalates back to FAIL on its own, so
#   the state can never quietly become permanent. BLOCKED does not raise the critical alert;
#   everything that is not PASS or BLOCKED does.
#
#   NOT covered, deliberately: "the agent gave a GOOD answer". Unprovable here, and pretending
#   otherwise is how a checker starts lying.
#
# 🔴 FAIL CLOSED. An unreachable host, a probe that times out, a registry that will not parse:
#   FAIL or ERROR, never PASS. Silence is never evidence of health.
#
# 🔴 IT READS THE REGISTRY. Adding a row to agents.yml puts that row under test with no edit to
#   this file -- the principle already stated at the top of agents.yml. A row that brings no
#   `health:` probe gets SKIP on A4 and its ROUTE verdict degrades to SKIP, so a new agent can
#   never inherit a green light it has not earned.
#
# 🔴 IT NEVER TOUCHES DISPLAY :0 AND NEVER TOUCHES THE CLIPBOARD.
#   - It never runs a capture tool: the round trip uses `ask --none`, which grabs nothing.
#   - It never spawns a terminal: it uses `ask --dry-open` and runs the printed command itself.
#   - It unsets DISPLAY and WAYLAND_DISPLAY for the `ask` child, so ask's xclip/wl-copy branch
#     finds no display and exits instantly, leaving Chris's clipboard alone. (Measured
#     2026-09-07: a stray detached xclip from an earlier test owned his clipboard for an hour.)
#
# usage:
#   ask-selftest                     cheap (default): CLASS A only. No LLM spend. Safe to cron.
#   ask-selftest --full              CLASS A + CLASS B. Spends LLM calls. Skips `paid: true`
#                                    rows unless --paid is given too.
#   ask-selftest --full --paid       ...including metered rows (hermes bills a real account).
#   ask-selftest --only claude,codex  limit to these ids      --skip hermes   exclude these ids
#   ask-selftest --registry PATH     test a fixture instead of the live registry
#   ask-selftest --quiet             summary + verdict only
import argparse, calendar, datetime, getpass, os, random, re, shutil, socket, subprocess, sys, time

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
DEFAULT_REG = os.environ.get("ASKBAR_REGISTRY", os.path.join(HERE, "agents.yml"))

PASS, FAIL, SKIP, ERROR, BLOCKED = "PASS", "FAIL", "SKIP", "ERROR", "BLOCKED"
# Worst-wins, ONE order used everywhere -- per check, per route, and for the overall verdict.
# BLOCKED sits ABOVE PASS (it is not health) and BELOW SKIP, deliberately: a SKIP means some
# route is NOT BEING CHECKED, which is the condition that let three routes stay dead for a day,
# and it must never be masked by a route that is merely waiting on a human.
RANK = {PASS: 0, BLOCKED: 1, SKIP: 2, FAIL: 3, ERROR: 4}
DEFAULT_GRACE_DAYS = 14

SSH_BASE = ["ssh", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", "ConnectTimeout=8",
            "-o", "StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new"]


DEFAULT_AGENT_TIMEOUT = 240


def run(argv, timeout):
    """Run argv, return (status_word, combined_output). status_word is TIMEOUT/RAN/NOEXEC.
    The RETURN CODE IS DELIBERATELY NOT RETURNED: every verdict in this file is decided by
    matching printed text, per the fleet rule that exit codes lie (ssh exits with the REMOTE
    command's status, so 'not found' and 'host down' are indistinguishable by $?)."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(argv, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=timeout)
        return "RAN", p.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace")
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
        got = (e.stdout or b"").decode("utf-8", "replace")
        return "TIMEOUT", got
    except OSError as e:
        return "NOEXEC", str(e)


def local_ips():
    _, out = run(["ip", "-4", "-o", "addr", "show"], 10)
    return {ln.split()[3].split("/")[0] for ln in out.splitlines() if len(ln.split()) > 3}


def ssh_resolved_host(alias):
    """A registry host is an SSH ALIAS ('hub'), which DNS cannot resolve -- it exists only
    in ~/.ssh/config. `ssh -G` reads that same config and prints the real hostname without
    connecting. Ported from ask's is_local_host() so the two agree about which leg is under
    test; a checker that disagrees with the program tests a leg the program never uses."""
    _, out = run(["ssh", "-G", alias], 10)
    for ln in out.splitlines():
        if ln.startswith("hostname "):
            return ln.split(None, 1)[1].strip()
    return ""


_LOCAL_CACHE = {}


def is_local_host(h):
    h = h.split("@")[-1]
    if h in _LOCAL_CACHE:
        return _LOCAL_CACHE[h]
    ans = False
    if h in ("local", "localhost"):
        ans = True
    else:
        hn = socket.gethostname()
        if h == hn or h == hn.split(".")[0]:
            ans = True
        else:
            mine = local_ips()
            for name in {h, ssh_resolved_host(h)}:
                if not name:
                    continue
                try:
                    addrs = {a[4][0] for a in socket.getaddrinfo(name, None, socket.AF_INET)}
                except OSError:
                    addrs = set()
                if name in mine:
                    addrs.add(name)
                if addrs & mine:
                    ans = True
                    break
    _LOCAL_CACHE[h] = ans
    return ans


def on_host(host, cmd, timeout):
    """Run one shell command where the route would run it. Remote goes through the SAME
    non-interactive `ssh host "cmd"` shape ask builds -- that shape's thin PATH is the whole
    of F1, so testing any other shape would test a machine that is not the one in play."""
    if is_local_host(host):
        return run(["bash", "-c", cmd], timeout)
    return run(SSH_BASE + [host, cmd], timeout)


class Check(object):
    def __init__(self, name, verdict, detail):
        self.name, self.verdict, self.detail = name, verdict, detail


def check_shape(row):
    """A1. The same invariants _regcheck.py enforces file-wide, asserted for THIS row so the
    per-route line says which row is malformed rather than only that the file is."""
    bad = []
    drop = row.get("drop")
    if not isinstance(drop, str) or ":" not in drop:
        bad.append("drop has no HOST: prefix")
    else:
        path = drop.split(":", 1)[1]
        if not path.startswith("/"):
            bad.append("drop path %r is not absolute (it would resolve against the agent's cwd)" % path)
    cmd = row.get("ask_cmd")
    if not isinstance(cmd, str) or not cmd.strip():
        bad.append("ask_cmd is empty")
    else:
        if "{q}" not in cmd:
            bad.append("ask_cmd has no {q} placeholder")
        first = cmd.split(" ", 1)[0]
        if not first.startswith("/"):
            bad.append("ask_cmd starts with %r, a bare name -- not on the non-interactive PATH" % first)
    v = row.get("vision")
    if not isinstance(v, bool):
        # 🔴 WORDING CORRECTED 2026-09-07 (stage 4). This used to say a quoted "false" "reads as
        # TRUE and ships a .png". That was true of the ask of that morning and is NOT true now:
        # _reg.py coerces to a canonical bool and fails closed, so a quoted value means NO image
        # whichever way it is spelled. Leaving the old sentence here would have been a warning
        # that outlived its defect -- the exact hazard CLAUDE.md Rule 4 records, where a stale
        # note about a repaired instrument made a later session build duplicate machinery.
        bad.append("vision is %s %r, not a YAML bool. It now fails CLOSED (no image, whichever "
                   "way it is spelled) and ask says so out loud, but the row is still lying "
                   "about the agent's capability and the next reader will trust it"
                   % (type(v).__name__, v))
    a_ssh = row.get("agent_ssh")
    if not isinstance(a_ssh, str) or "@" not in a_ssh or not a_ssh.split("@")[-1].strip():
        bad.append("agent_ssh %r is not USER@HOST -- without it there is no way to ask whether "
                   "the drop lands on the machine the agent reads on, which is the whole of the "
                   "2026-09-07 F2 bug" % a_ssh)
    if isinstance(drop, str) and "'" in drop:
        bad.append("drop contains a single quote; it is interpolated into a remote shell command")
    if bad:
        return Check("shape", FAIL, "; ".join(bad))
    return Check("shape", PASS, "drop %s absolute, ask_cmd absolute + {q}, vision is a bool, "
                                "agent_ssh %s" % (drop, a_ssh))


def check_drop(row, timeout):
    """A2. The drop is where the question physically lands. Unreachable host = FAIL, not a
    shrug: the capture would be lost and ask would have nothing to hand over."""
    drop = row.get("drop") or ""
    if ":" not in drop:
        return Check("drop-reach", FAIL, "unparseable drop %r" % drop)
    host, path = drop.split(":", 1)
    probe = ('if [ -d "%s" ] && [ -w "%s" ]; then echo DROP_DIR_WRITABLE; '
             'elif [ -d "%s" ]; then echo DROP_DIR_READONLY; '
             'else echo DROP_DIR_MISSING; fi' % (path, path, path))
    st, out = on_host(host, probe, timeout)
    if st == "TIMEOUT":
        return Check("drop-reach", FAIL, "%s did not answer within %ss -- fail closed" % (host, timeout))
    if "DROP_DIR_WRITABLE" in out:
        return Check("drop-reach", PASS, "%s:%s exists and is writable by the account ask copies as" % (host, path))
    if "DROP_DIR_READONLY" in out:
        return Check("drop-reach", FAIL, "%s:%s exists but is NOT writable -- the drop would be refused" % (host, path))
    if "DROP_DIR_MISSING" in out:
        return Check("drop-reach", FAIL, "%s:%s does not exist" % (host, path))
    return Check("drop-reach", FAIL, "cannot reach %s (no probe word came back): %s"
                 % (host, " ".join(out.split())[:160] or "<no output>"))


def check_resolve(row, timeout):
    """A3. Exactly ask's dispatch preflight: does the executable resolve on ask_host, in the
    shell the dispatch uses. Matching __MISSING__ rather than $? is deliberate -- ssh returns
    the REMOTE status, so a dead hop and a missing binary are the same number."""
    host = row.get("ask_host") or "local"
    cmd = row.get("ask_cmd") or ""
    exe = cmd.split(" ", 1)[0]
    if not exe:
        return Check("cmd-resolve", FAIL, "ask_cmd is empty; there is nothing to resolve")
    # 🔴 BOTH OUTCOMES ARE POSITIVE WORDS, AND SILENCE IS THE THIRD. Caught by the deadhop
    # fixture 2026-09-07, in this file: the first version asked `command -v EXE || echo
    # __MISSING__` and treated "no __MISSING__" as success. Against an unreachable host that is
    # exactly what comes back -- ssh's own "connect to host 10.255.255.1 port 22: Connection
    # timed out" on stderr, which this function merges into stdout -- so a black hole read
    # cmd-resolve PASS. `ask` gets away with the same shape only because it discards stderr and
    # tests for an EMPTY string; relying on emptiness is one redirection away from breaking.
    # Requiring __FOUND__ cannot be satisfied by an error message, so it fails closed by
    # construction rather than by care.
    st, out = on_host(host, "command -v '%s' >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo __FOUND__ || echo __MISSING__" % exe, timeout)
    if st == "TIMEOUT":
        return Check("cmd-resolve", FAIL, "%s did not answer within %ss -- the session would open onto a dead hop" % (host, timeout))
    if "__FOUND__" in out:
        return Check("cmd-resolve", PASS, "%s resolves on %s" % (exe, host))
    if "__MISSING__" in out:
        return Check("cmd-resolve", FAIL, "%s cannot run %s -- not found on that host's non-interactive PATH" % (host, exe))
    return Check("cmd-resolve", FAIL, "cannot reach %s -- no verdict word came back, so the session "
                 "would open onto a dead hop: %s" % (host, " ".join(out.split())[:140] or "<no output>"))


def _addrs(name):
    """Every IPv4 address a host name / ssh alias resolves to, so two spellings of one machine
    compare equal. `gpu-box` and `gpuuser@192.0.2.82` are the same box; string equality
    says they are not, and that mistake is exactly what the registry could not previously see."""
    name = name.split("@")[-1]
    out = set()
    for n in {name, ssh_resolved_host(name)}:
        if not n:
            continue
        try:
            out |= {a[4][0] for a in socket.getaddrinfo(n, None, socket.AF_INET)}
        except OSError:
            pass
    if is_local_host(name):
        out |= local_ips()
    return out


def _as_agent(a_ssh, cmd, timeout):
    """Run a command AS THE AGENT'S ACCOUNT, on the agent's machine.

    🔴 CAUGHT BY ITS OWN HEALTHY-CONTROL FIXTURE, 2026-09-07, minutes after this check was
    written: the first version always went through ssh, so when the agent host was THIS machine
    it tried to ssh the laptop to itself and got `Permission denied (publickey)` -- a FAIL that
    said "the agent cannot read the drop" when the truth was "I cannot log in to myself". A
    check that fails closed for the wrong reason is still a check that lies.
    But going local whenever the HOST matches is also wrong, and wrong in the more dangerous
    direction: `agent_ssh` names a USER as well as a host, and on the Mini the writer is
    the box owner while the reader is the agent account. Running as ourselves there would
    measure the wrong account and report a read the agent does not have. So: local only when the
    host is this machine AND the account is this account; ssh otherwise, even to localhost."""
    user, _, host = a_ssh.partition("@")
    if host and is_local_host(host) and user == getpass.getuser():
        return run(["bash", "-c", cmd], timeout)
    return run(SSH_BASE + [a_ssh, cmd], timeout)


def check_agentside(row, timeout):
    """A5. TWO CLAIMS, BOTH MEASURED, NEITHER PREVIOUSLY ASKED.

    (i) the drop host and `agent_ssh` are THE SAME MACHINE. On 2026-09-07 a bridged row dropped
        on the bridge host while the agent ran on another box; shape, drop-reach, cmd-resolve and
        cmd-health all said PASS, because every one of them was asking about a different box
        than the one holding the reader. Only an LLM round trip could see it. This sees it free.
    (ii) that account can actually READ the drop. Permissions and ACLs are not the same question
        as "is the directory writable by the account ask copies as" -- the Hermes spine inbox is
        the case in point: the box owner writes it, the agent account reads it, and the read is
        granted by an ACL rather than by the POSIX bits."""
    drop = row.get("drop") or ""
    a_ssh = row.get("agent_ssh") or ""
    if ":" not in drop or "@" not in a_ssh:
        return Check("drop-agentside", FAIL, "unusable drop %r / agent_ssh %r" % (drop, a_ssh))
    dhost, path = drop.split(":", 1)
    da, aa = _addrs(dhost), _addrs(a_ssh)
    if not da or not aa:
        return Check("drop-agentside", FAIL,
                     "cannot resolve %s (%s) or %s (%s) to an address -- fail closed rather than "
                     "assume they are the same machine" % (dhost, sorted(da), a_ssh, sorted(aa)))
    if not (da & aa):
        return Check("drop-agentside", FAIL,
                     "THE FILE LANDS ON A DIFFERENT MACHINE THAN THE AGENT READS ON: drop host %s "
                     "%s vs agent %s %s. This is the 2026-09-07 F2 bug exactly; every other CLASS "
                     "A check can pass while it is true." % (dhost, sorted(da), a_ssh, sorted(aa)))
    probe = ("if [ ! -d '%s' ]; then echo AGENT_DIR_MISSING; else "
             "if [ -r '%s' ] && [ -x '%s' ]; then echo AGENT_DIR_LISTABLE; "
             "else echo AGENT_DIR_UNLISTABLE; fi; "
             "f=''; for c in '%s'/*; do [ -f \"$c\" ] && f=\"$c\"; done; "
             "if [ -n \"$f\" ]; then if [ -r \"$f\" ]; then echo AGENT_FILE_READABLE; "
             "else echo AGENT_FILE_UNREADABLE; fi; else echo AGENT_DIR_EMPTY; fi; fi"
             % (path, path, path, path))
    st, out = _as_agent(a_ssh, probe, timeout)
    if st == "TIMEOUT":
        return Check("drop-agentside", FAIL, "%s did not answer within %ss -- fail closed" % (a_ssh, timeout))
    if "AGENT_DIR_MISSING" in out:
        return Check("drop-agentside", FAIL, "%s cannot see %s at all" % (a_ssh, path))
    if "AGENT_DIR_UNLISTABLE" in out:
        return Check("drop-agentside", FAIL, "%s cannot list %s -- the drop would land where the "
                                             "agent cannot open it" % (a_ssh, path))
    if "AGENT_FILE_UNREADABLE" in out:
        return Check("drop-agentside", FAIL, "%s can list %s but cannot READ a file in it" % (a_ssh, path))
    if "AGENT_DIR_LISTABLE" in out and "AGENT_FILE_READABLE" in out:
        return Check("drop-agentside", PASS, "same machine as the drop, and %s can read files in "
                                             "%s (verified against a real file, not assumed)" % (a_ssh, path))
    if "AGENT_DIR_LISTABLE" in out and "AGENT_DIR_EMPTY" in out:
        return Check("drop-agentside", PASS, "same machine as the drop, and %s can list %s "
                                             "(directory is empty, so no file read was proven)" % (a_ssh, path))
    return Check("drop-agentside", FAIL, "no verdict word came back from %s: %s"
                 % (a_ssh, " ".join(out.split())[:140] or "<no output>"))


def _blocked(p, why):
    """A probe that FAILED but declares `blocked_on:`. Returns (verdict, note).
    The clock is the point: BLOCKED with no expiry is a mute button, so past the grace period it
    escalates back to FAIL and starts raising the alert again."""
    since = str(p.get("blocked_since", ""))
    try:
        d = datetime.date(*[int(x) for x in since.split("-")])
    except Exception:
        # FAIL CLOSED. A blocked state whose clock cannot be read is a blocked state that can
        # never expire, which is the failure this whole mechanism exists to avoid.
        return FAIL, ("declares blocked_on but blocked_since %r is unusable, so the wait could "
                      "never expire -- refusing to soften the verdict || underlying: %s"
                      % (since, why))
    days = (datetime.date.today() - d).days
    grace = p.get("blocked_grace_days", DEFAULT_GRACE_DAYS)
    if not isinstance(grace, int) or isinstance(grace, bool) or grace < 1:
        grace = DEFAULT_GRACE_DAYS
    if days <= grace:
        return BLOCKED, ("BLOCKED-ON-CHRIS %d day(s) (grace %d): %s || underlying: %s"
                         % (days, grace, p["blocked_on"], why))
    return FAIL, ("was BLOCKED-ON-CHRIS but has waited %d days, past its %d-day grace -- "
                  "escalated to FAIL so it stops being invisible: %s || underlying: %s"
                  % (days, grace, p["blocked_on"], why))


def check_health(row, timeout):
    """A4. THE CHECK STAGE 1 DID NOT HAVE, and its absence is why two dead routes read green.
    `health:` is a LIST of {what, cmd, ok} taken from the ROW, so it is not a claim written in a
    field -- it is a command that runs and text that must come back. `ok` is a Python regex
    matched with DOTALL over stdout+stderr. POSITIVE matching only: requiring a word that
    healthy output contains beats blacklisting error words, because a program that dies before
    printing anything matches no blacklist at all."""
    probes = row.get("health")
    if not probes:
        return Check("cmd-health", SKIP,
                     "row declares no `health:` probe -- `command -v` proves the NAME resolves, "
                     "not that the program STARTS (codex --version exits 0 with a broken config)")
    if not isinstance(probes, list):
        return Check("cmd-health", ERROR, "`health:` is %s, expected a list" % type(probes).__name__)
    host = row.get("ask_host") or "local"
    notes = []
    worst = PASS
    for p in probes:
        what = (p or {}).get("what", "?")
        cmd = (p or {}).get("cmd")
        ok = (p or {}).get("ok")
        if not cmd or not ok:
            notes.append("%s: ERROR (probe needs both cmd and ok)" % what)
            worst = ERROR if RANK[ERROR] > RANK[worst] else worst
            continue
        # 🔴 A PROBE MUST NOT MATCH ITS OWN INPUT. Caught by measurement 2026-09-07, in a probe
        # written FOR THIS FILE an hour earlier: `codex exec ... 'Reply with exactly: CODEXPROBE'`
        # with ok=/CODEXPROBE/ reported PASS while the API was refusing the request outright --
        # because codex ECHOES THE USER PROMPT (line 14 of its own transcript) and the word the
        # probe wanted was sitting in that echo. Green by construction, inside the very check
        # built to end green-by-construction. The rule is now mechanical: if `ok` matches the
        # command string itself, the probe proves nothing and is ERROR, not PASS. A probe whose
        # word legitimately appears in its own command -- a shell that decides locally and
        # echoes a verdict word -- must say so with `ok_in_cmd: true`, which is deliberate,
        # visible and countable rather than assumed.
        if not p.get("ok_in_cmd") and re.search(ok, cmd, re.S):
            notes.append("%s: ERROR (/%s/ matches the COMMAND ITSELF, so it would pass on the "
                         "echo of its own input and prove nothing; fix the probe, or set "
                         "ok_in_cmd: true if the command really does decide locally)" % (what, ok))
            worst = ERROR if RANK[ERROR] > RANK[worst] else worst
            continue
        st, out = on_host(host, cmd, timeout)
        if st == "TIMEOUT":
            notes.append("%s: FAIL (no answer in %ss)" % (what, timeout))
            worst = FAIL if RANK[FAIL] > RANK[worst] else worst
            continue
        if re.search(ok, out, re.S):
            notes.append("%s: PASS (matched /%s/)" % (what, ok))
        else:
            first = " ".join(out.split())[:150] or "<no output>"
            why = "/%s/ absent; got: %s" % (ok, first)
            if p.get("blocked_on"):
                # 🔴 A ROUTE WAITING ON A HUMAN IS NOT A BROKEN ROUTE. Reporting both as FAIL is
                # how a real breakage hides inside a backlog Chris has already been told about.
                # _regcheck.py refuses a blocked_on with no blocked_since, so the clock below
                # always exists and the state always expires.
                v, note = _blocked(p, why)
                notes.append("%s: %s (%s)" % (what, v, note))
                worst = v if RANK[v] > RANK[worst] else worst
            else:
                notes.append("%s: FAIL (%s)" % (what, why))
                worst = FAIL if RANK[FAIL] > RANK[worst] else worst
    return Check("cmd-health", worst, " | ".join(notes))


TOKEN_ALPHABET = "ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTUVWXYZ23456789"   # no I L O, same rule as a SID suffix


def check_roundtrip(row, args, health=None):
    """B1. THE ONLY CHECK THAT PROVES THE AGENT READ THE FILE.
    It drives the REAL `ask`, so the drop, the sha verification, the vision gate, the sidecar
    and the exact prompt string all come from the program under test rather than from a copy
    of it here. Only the invocation differs -- `test_cmd` is the row's own one-shot form,
    because ask_cmd opens an interactive REPL that a checker can never read."""
    rid = row["id"]
    # 🔴 A BLOCKED ROUTE MUST NOT BE ROUND-TRIPPED, AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT PROVED IT IS BELOW.
    # Live run 2026-09-07 18:48Z: codex reported cmd-health BLOCKED (waiting on Chris for a trust
    # decision) and then round-trip FAIL -- "Not inside a trusted directory". The round trip was
    # not discovering a second fault; it was re-discovering the FIRST one, and because FAIL
    # outranks BLOCKED the route came out FAIL. That silently undid the whole point of having a
    # third word: the one route that was genuinely broken would have been indistinguishable from
    # the two that are merely waiting on a human. Same for continue.
    # So: if the route is already known to be waiting on a person, say so and spend nothing. The
    # honest answer and the cheap answer are the same answer here.
    if health is not None and health.verdict == BLOCKED:
        return Check("round-trip", BLOCKED,
                     "not attempted -- this route is BLOCKED-ON-CHRIS upstream (see cmd-health). "
                     "A round trip could only re-discover that, and it would report it as FAIL, "
                     "which is precisely the collapse the BLOCKED word exists to prevent")
    test_cmd = row.get("test_cmd")
    if not test_cmd:
        return Check("round-trip", SKIP, "row declares no `test_cmd:` (a NON-INTERACTIVE one-shot "
                                         "form); ask_cmd opens a REPL and cannot be scored")
    if row.get("paid") and not args.paid:
        return Check("round-trip", SKIP, "row is `paid: true` (it bills a real account); pass --paid to spend one call")

    token = "SELFTEST-" + "".join(random.choice(TOKEN_ALPHABET) for _ in range(6))
    a, b = random.randint(11, 89), random.randint(11, 89)
    question = (
        "ASK-BAR SELF-TEST. Do not explain, do not ask anything back. Reply with exactly "
        "two lines:\n"
        "TOKEN: %s\n"
        "SUM: %d\n"
        "where the SUM is %d plus %d. The token above is the one written in this file." 
        % (token, a + b, a, b))

    # 🔴 THE CHILD MUST NOT SEE A DISPLAY. ask's clipboard branch takes the first of wl-copy /
    # xclip it finds and DETACHES it (it has to: an X selection is served by a live process).
    # With no DISPLAY that branch dies instantly and Chris's clipboard is untouched. --none
    # means no capture tool runs at all, so nothing can take an X grab either.
    env = dict(os.environ)
    for k in ("DISPLAY", "WAYLAND_DISPLAY"):
        env.pop(k, None)
    env["ASKBAR_REGISTRY"] = args.registry
    ask = os.path.join(HERE, "ask")
    if not os.access(ask, os.X_OK):
        return Check("round-trip", ERROR, "no executable ask beside this script at %s" % ask)
    try:
        p = subprocess.run([ask, "--to", rid, "--none", "--text", question, "--dry-open"],
                           stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=args.timeout, env=env)
        ask_out = p.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace")
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return Check("round-trip", FAIL, "ask did not return within %ss" % args.timeout)

    # ask's own verdict word decides, never its exit status (it exits 0 on every path by design).
    verdict = ask_out.strip().split("\n")[0] if ask_out.strip() else "<no output>"
    if not verdict.startswith("OK"):
        return Check("round-trip", FAIL, "ask refused before dispatch: %s" % " ".join(ask_out.split())[:200])

    m = re.search(r"DRY-OPEN would run:\s*\n\s*(.+)", ask_out)
    if not m:
        return Check("round-trip", ERROR, "ask printed OK but no DRY-OPEN line to take the prompt from")
    dry = m.group(1).strip()
    # Take the prompt VERBATIM from ask's own output rather than rebuilding it here. A second
    # copy of that string is a transcription, and a transcription is a bug with a delay fuse
    # (CLAUDE.md Rule 4). The prompt is the last single-quoted run in the dispatch line.
    quoted = re.findall(r"'((?:[^']|'\\'')*)'", dry)
    if not quoted:
        return Check("round-trip", ERROR, "could not read the prompt back out of: %s" % dry[:160])
    prompt = quoted[-1].replace("'\\''", "'")
    if token in prompt:
        return Check("round-trip", ERROR, "the token leaked into the COMMAND LINE; the agent could "
                                          "answer without opening the file, so this proves nothing")

    # --- B2: THE DECOY DISCRIMINATOR --------------------------------------------------------
    # 🔴 THE TOKEN PROVES THE AGENT OPENED *A* FILE. IT DOES NOT PROVE IT OPENED *OURS*.
    # That distinction is not academic: on 2026-09-07 dreamzs was handed a relative path that
    # did not resolve, SEARCHED, found the file anyway, and answered correctly -- and that
    # recovery is precisely what hid a broken route for a day. So two decoys, carrying tokens
    # that appear nowhere else, are written into the SAME directory AFTER the real drop (so they
    # are the newest files there) and are never named to the agent. Decoy content in the answer
    # is proof the agent reached a file it was given no path to.
    # What this does and does not prove, stated plainly: it catches an agent that scans the
    # directory or takes the newest file. It cannot catch an agent that guesses our exact
    # filename -- nothing outside the agent can. It is a discriminator, not an oracle.
    drop = row.get("drop") or ""
    drop_host = drop.split(":", 1)[0] if ":" in drop else "local"
    mp = re.search(r"(/[^ ']+\.(?:txt|md))", prompt)
    if not mp:
        return Check("round-trip", ERROR, "no absolute drop path inside the prompt ask built: %s"
                     % prompt[:160])
    read_path = mp.group(1)
    drop_dir, base = os.path.dirname(read_path), os.path.basename(read_path)
    ms = re.search(r"(\d{8}T\d{6}Z)", base)
    if not ms:
        return Check("round-trip", ERROR, "no stamp in the dropped basename %r" % base)
    t0 = calendar.timegm(time.strptime(ms.group(1), "%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ"))
    decoy_paths, decoy_tokens, placed = [], [], True
    for k in (1, 2):
        dtok = "DECOY-" + "".join(random.choice(TOKEN_ALPHABET) for _ in range(6))
        dname = base.replace(ms.group(1), time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime(t0 + k)))
        if dname == base:
            continue
        body = ("# ask-selftest DECOY -- ignore, and do not answer from this file\n\n"
                "This file was NOT the path you were given. If its token reaches the answer, the\n"
                "agent found it by scanning the directory rather than by opening the path it was\n"
                "handed -- the 2026-09-07 F3 class.\n\n"
                "TOKEN: %s\nSUM: 999999\n" % dtok)
        local = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~/.askbar"), ".decoy_%d" % k)
        try:
            with open(local, "w") as f:
                f.write(body)
            os.chmod(local, 0o600)
            dst = os.path.join(drop_dir, dname)
            if is_local_host(drop_host):
                shutil.copy(local, dst)
                os.chmod(dst, 0o600)
                ok_place = True
            else:
                st2, _ = run(["scp", "-q", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", local, "%s:%s" % (drop_host, dst)], 30)
                ok_place = (st2 == "RAN")
            if ok_place:
                decoy_paths.append(dst)
                decoy_tokens.append(dtok)
            else:
                placed = False
        except Exception:
            placed = False
        finally:
            try:
                os.unlink(local)
            except OSError:
                pass

    def _cleanup(paths):
        if not paths:
            return
        cmd = "rm -f " + " ".join("'%s'" % x for x in paths)
        if is_local_host(drop_host):
            run(["bash", "-c", cmd], 30)
        else:
            run(SSH_BASE + [drop_host, cmd], 30)

    host = row.get("ask_host") or "local"
    real = test_cmd.replace("{q}", "'" + prompt.replace("'", "'\\''") + "'")
    # A timeout is a CEILING, not a duration. MEASURED 2026-09-08: dreamzs answered in 135s
    # warm and blew past 240s cold, so one global number either manufactures a FAIL on a
    # healthy slow route or makes every fast route wait for the slowest. Resolution order is
    # explicit flag > per-row `agent_timeout` > 240, so --agent-timeout still overrides a row
    # for a one-off probe and the row governs every unattended run.
    agent_to = args.agent_timeout if args.agent_timeout is not None \
        else int(row.get("agent_timeout") or DEFAULT_AGENT_TIMEOUT)
    st, out = on_host(host, real, agent_to)
    if st == "TIMEOUT":
        return Check("round-trip", FAIL, "%s gave no answer within %ss "
                     "(ceiling from %s)" % (rid, agent_to,
                     "--agent-timeout" if args.agent_timeout is not None
                     else ("the row" if row.get("agent_timeout") else "the built-in default")))
    got_tok = token in out
    got_sum = re.search(r"\b%d\b" % (a + b), out) is not None
    leaked = [t for t in decoy_tokens if t in out]
    first = " ".join(out.split())[:200] or "<no output>"
    dnote = ("%d decoy file(s) placed" % len(decoy_tokens)) if decoy_tokens else "NO DECOY PLACED"
    if got_tok and got_sum:
        _cleanup(decoy_paths + [read_path])
    else:
        _cleanup(decoy_paths)          # leave the real drop behind as evidence of a failure
    if leaked:
        return Check("round-trip", FAIL,
                     "PATH FIDELITY FAILED -- the answer contains %s, which exists ONLY in a decoy "
                     "file this checker never named. The agent reached a file it was given no path "
                     "to (it scanned the directory, or took the newest file). That is the "
                     "2026-09-07 F3 class, and it is exactly how a broken route passed for a day. "
                     "|| reply: %s" % (", ".join(leaked), first))
    if got_tok and got_sum:
        # 🔴 DO NOT CLAIM PATH FIDELITY WHEN NO DECOY WAS PLACED. Found by attacking this
        # function's own output rather than its inputs: if the copy failed -- read-only drop,
        # a full disk, an ssh blip -- decoy_tokens is empty, `leaked` is trivially empty, and
        # the old wording still read "with NOTHING from the decoy files". That is a claim about
        # evidence that was never gathered, which is the exact species of statement this whole
        # day has been spent removing. Two different PASSes, because they rest on two different
        # amounts of proof.
        if not decoy_tokens:
            return Check("round-trip", PASS,
                         "answered with the token %s AND the computed sum %d -- it opened A file "
                         "it was not given the content of. ⚠️ PATH FIDELITY NOT TESTED: no decoy "
                         "could be placed in %s, so nothing here distinguishes 'read the path we "
                         "named' from 'found the file some other way'" % (token, a + b, drop_dir))
        return Check("round-trip", PASS,
                     "answered with the token %s AND the computed sum %d, and with NOTHING from "
                     "the %s sitting newer in the same directory -- so it opened the path it was "
                     "given, not whatever it found there" % (token, a + b, dnote))
    missing = []
    if not got_tok:
        missing.append("token %s absent (it never opened the file, or opened the wrong path)" % token)
    if not got_sum:
        missing.append("sum %d absent (it did not read the numbers)" % (a + b))
    if not placed:
        missing.append("NOTE: a decoy could not be placed, so path fidelity was NOT tested")
    return Check("round-trip", FAIL, "%s || reply: %s" % ("; ".join(missing), first))


def regcheck(registry, timeout):
    script = os.path.join(HERE, "_regcheck.py")
    if not os.path.exists(script):
        return ERROR, "no _regcheck.py beside this script"
    st, out = run(["python3", script, registry], timeout)
    if st == "TIMEOUT":
        return ERROR, "_regcheck.py did not finish"
    line = out.strip().split("\n")[0] if out.strip() else "<no output>"
    if out.lstrip().startswith("PASS"):
        return PASS, line
    if out.lstrip().startswith("FAIL"):
        return FAIL, out.strip()
    return ERROR, "_regcheck.py printed no PASS/FAIL word: %s" % line


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=True)
    ap.add_argument("--registry", default=DEFAULT_REG)
    ap.add_argument("--full", action="store_true",
                    help="also run the canary round trip (CLASS B). Spends LLM calls.")
    ap.add_argument("--paid", action="store_true",
                    help="with --full, also spend on rows marked `paid: true`")
    ap.add_argument("--only", default="", help="comma-separated agent ids to test")
    ap.add_argument("--skip", default="", help="comma-separated agent ids to leave out")
    ap.add_argument("--timeout", type=int, default=45, help="seconds for a cheap probe")
    ap.add_argument("--agent-timeout", type=int, default=None,
                    help="seconds to wait for an agent's answer; overrides a row's "
                         "agent_timeout. Unset: the row's value, else %d" % DEFAULT_AGENT_TIMEOUT)
    ap.add_argument("--quiet", action="store_true")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    try:
        import yaml
    except ImportError:
        print("ERROR — python3 yaml is not installed; the registry cannot be read")
        print("SELFTEST VERDICT: ERROR")
        return
    started = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    mode = "full" + ("+paid" if args.paid else "") if args.full else "cheap"
    out = []

    def say(s):
        out.append(s)
        if not args.quiet:
            print(s)
            sys.stdout.flush()

    say("ask-selftest %s  mode=%s  host=%s  registry=%s" % (started, mode, socket.gethostname(), args.registry))
    if not args.full:
        say("  cheap mode proves CLASS A only: the command can run. It does NOT prove any agent")
        say("  read the dropped file -- that is CLASS B and needs --full.")

    rc_word, rc_detail = regcheck(args.registry, args.timeout)
    say("REGISTRY  %-5s  %s" % (rc_word, rc_detail))

    try:
        with open(args.registry) as f:
            doc = yaml.safe_load(f)
        rows = doc["agents"]
        if not isinstance(rows, list):
            raise ValueError("'agents:' is not a list")
    except Exception as e:
        say("ERROR — cannot read %s: %s" % (args.registry, e))
        say("SUMMARY  mode=%s  routes=0  PASS=0  FAIL=0  SKIP=0  ERROR=1  ALERT=YES" % mode)
        say("SELFTEST VERDICT: ERROR")
        _persist(out, args)
        return

    only = {x for x in args.only.split(",") if x}
    skipped = {x for x in args.skip.split(",") if x}
    tally = {PASS: 0, FAIL: 0, SKIP: 0, ERROR: 0, BLOCKED: 0}

    for row in rows:
        if not isinstance(row, dict):
            say("")
            say("route <unparseable>")
            say("  ROUTE <unparseable>  ERROR  a registry entry is not a mapping (indentation collapsed it)")
            tally[ERROR] += 1
            continue
        rid = row.get("id", "<no id>")
        say("")
        say("route %s  (%s)" % (rid, row.get("label", "?")))
        if (only and rid not in only) or rid in skipped:
            say("  ROUTE %-10s %-5s  excluded by --only/--skip" % (rid, SKIP))
            tally[SKIP] += 1
            continue
        checks = [check_shape(row)]
        if checks[0].verdict == PASS:
            checks.append(check_drop(row, args.timeout))
            checks.append(check_agentside(row, args.timeout))
            checks.append(check_resolve(row, args.timeout))
            checks.append(check_health(row, args.timeout))
            if args.full:
                checks.append(check_roundtrip(row, args, health=checks[-1]))
        else:
            # A malformed row must not be probed: every probe below reads a field that the shape
            # check just said cannot be trusted, and a probe on a bad value reports a confident
            # wrong answer. Say so rather than skipping silently.
            checks.append(Check("drop-reach", SKIP, "not probed: the row's own shape is wrong"))
            checks.append(Check("drop-agentside", SKIP, "not probed: the row's own shape is wrong"))
            checks.append(Check("cmd-resolve", SKIP, "not probed: the row's own shape is wrong"))
            checks.append(Check("cmd-health", SKIP, "not probed: the row's own shape is wrong"))
        worst = PASS
        for c in checks:
            say("  %-12s %-5s  %s" % (c.name, c.verdict, c.detail))
            if RANK[c.verdict] > RANK[worst]:
                worst = c.verdict
        # The scope note must describe what THIS route's verdict actually rests on, not what a
        # PASS would have meant in this mode. A BLOCKED route was never round-tripped -- saying
        # "the agent read the file" beside it would be a claim nothing measured.
        if worst == BLOCKED:
            scope = "waiting on Chris; no round trip was attempted, so nothing here is a claim about the agent"
        else:
            scope = ("command runs AND the agent read the file" if args.full
                     else "command can run; NOT proof the agent read the file")
        say("  ROUTE %-10s %-5s  (%s)" % (rid, worst, scope))
        tally[worst] += 1

    n = sum(tally.values())
    # BLOCKED does NOT raise the alert -- that is its whole purpose. SKIP still does: "nothing is
    # checking this route" is the condition that let three routes stay dead for a day.
    alert = "YES" if (tally[FAIL] or tally[ERROR] or tally[SKIP]) else "NO"
    say("")
    say("SUMMARY  mode=%s  routes=%d  PASS=%d  BLOCKED=%d  FAIL=%d  SKIP=%d  ERROR=%d  ALERT=%s"
        % (mode, n, tally[PASS], tally[BLOCKED], tally[FAIL], tally[SKIP], tally[ERROR], alert))
    if tally[BLOCKED]:
        say("BLOCKED-ON-CHRIS  %d route(s) are wired correctly and waiting on a human decision."
            % tally[BLOCKED])
        say("                  This is NOT the same state as BROKEN and NOT the same as PASS.")
        say("                  Each one escalates itself back to FAIL once past its grace period.")
    if rc_word in (FAIL, ERROR):
        overall = rc_word
    elif tally[ERROR]:
        overall = ERROR
    elif tally[FAIL]:
        overall = FAIL
    elif tally[SKIP]:
        overall = SKIP
    elif tally[BLOCKED]:
        overall = BLOCKED
    else:
        overall = PASS
    say("SELFTEST VERDICT: %s" % overall)
    _persist(out, args)


def _persist(lines, args):
    """Durable output (CLAUDE.md Rule 14): terminal scrollback is not evidence. The last run is
    always on disk, so a FAIL that fired at 08:20 is still readable at noon.

    🔴 A FIXTURE RUN MUST NOT OVERWRITE THE OPERATIONAL RECORD. Caught 2026-09-07 minutes after
    this file was written: ask-selftest-fixtures runs the checker ten times against deliberately
    broken COPIES of the registry, and every one of them clobbered ~/.askbar/selftest.log --
    which is the file ask-selftest-notify reads to name the routes that are down. Proving the
    checker still works would have made the next alert name the wrong routes. A run against
    anything but the live registry writes to selftest-fixtures.log instead."""
    # 🔴 A PARTIAL RUN IS NOT THE OPERATIONAL RECORD EITHER, AND THIS COST ME A WRONG ALERT
    # 2026-09-07: the original rule only redirected runs against a FIXTURE registry. A run
    # against the LIVE registry with `--only continue` still wrote selftest.log -- so the log
    # then held four SKIP lines, and ask-selftest-notify dutifully announced
    # "hermes, claude, codex, continue is DOWN" about a machine where nothing was down. The
    # routes were not down; they were simply not asked. Same failure as the fixture case, one
    # flag over: a DIAGNOSTIC run overwriting the record an ALARM reads.
    # The operational log is now written only by a run that actually covered every route.
    d = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".askbar")
    live = os.path.abspath(args.registry) == os.path.abspath(DEFAULT_REG)
    whole = not (args.only or args.skip)
    if live and whole:
        name = "selftest.log"
    elif live:
        name = "selftest-partial.log"
    else:
        name = "selftest-fixtures.log"
    try:
        os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
        with open(os.path.join(d, name), "w") as f:
            f.write("\n".join(lines) + "\n")
    except OSError:
        pass


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
